This note gives a conditional intersection argument. It does not assert the
centralizer dimension for any supplied tuple. The old tensor congruences and
middle dihedral group remain separately certified premises. The proposed new
calculation concerns the common commutant of two saved projections. No group
classification, orbit closure, or enumeration of its unit group is required.

\section{Conventions and the reduction to a commutant}
Vectors are rows. Let $Q=Q^{\mathsf T}\in\mathrm{GL}_n(K)$, where
$\operatorname{char}K\ne2$, and put
\[
 x^*=Qx^{\mathsf T}Q^{-1}.
\]
Thus $*$ is an involutory anti-automorphism, and the row isometry condition is
$xQx^{\mathsf T}=Q$, equivalently $xx^*=1$. If $A=A^{\mathsf T}$, then
$P=QA$ is self-adjoint: $P^*=P$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:covariance}
Suppose that $gQ g^{\mathsf T}=Q$. For a symmetric matrix $A$, the conditions
$g^{\mathsf T}Ag=A$ and $g(QA)=(QA)g$ are equivalent.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The isometry equation gives $gQ=Qg^{-\mathsf T}$. Consequently
$gQA=QAg$ is equivalent to $g^{-\mathsf T}A=Ag$, and hence to
$g^{\mathsf T}Ag=A$.
\end{proof}

Let $r_0,r_1,r_2,r_3\in\SO(Q)$, and set
$H=\langle r_0,r_1,r_2\rangle$ and $R=\langle r_1,r_2,r_3\rangle$.
Assume that symmetric saved tensors $A_H,A_R$ satisfy
\[
 r_i^{\mathsf T}A_Hr_i=A_H\quad(0\le i\le2),\qquad
 r_i^{\mathsf T}A_Rr_i=A_R\quad(1\le i\le3).
\]
The signs in these equations must be positive. Preservation up to a negative
sign does not give the commutation relation used below. Put
$P_H=QA_H$, $P_R=QA_R$, and
\[
 C=\{x\in\End_K(K^n):xP_H=P_Hx,\ xP_R=P_Rx\}.
\]
Lemma~\ref{lem:covariance} gives $H\cap R\subseteq\SO(Q)\cap C$.
The algebra $C$ is stable under $*$ because both $P_H$ and $P_R$ are
self-adjoint.

\section{The norm-one units}
The following elementary calculation is the algebraic part of the new
argument. It applies to either truncation length; neither dimension is presumed.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:units}
Let $C$ be a $K$-algebra with involution $*$. Suppose $B,N\in C$ and
$m\in\{2,3\}$ satisfy
\[
 B^2=1,\qquad BN=-NB,\qquad N^m=0,\qquad B^*=B,\quad N^*=-N,
\]
and that the following list is a basis of $C$:
\[
 \mathcal B_m=(1,B,N,BN,\ldots,N^{m-1},BN^{m-1}).
\]
Write $E_2(t)=1+tN$ and $E_3(t)=1+tN+\tfrac12t^2N^2$.
Then
\[
 \{x\in C:x^*x=1\}=\{\delta B^\epsilon E_m(t):
       \delta\in\{1,-1\},\ \epsilon\in\{0,1\},\ t\in K\}.
\]
All these expressions are distinct.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $J$ be the span of the basis elements containing a positive power of $N$.
The multiplication relations show that $J$ is a nilpotent, $*$-stable two-sided
ideal, and that $C/J$ has basis $1,B$ with $B^2=1$ and $B^*=B$.
If $x^*x=1$ and $x+J=a+bB$, reduction modulo $J$ gives
\[
 (a^2+b^2)1+2abB=1.
\]
Independence of $1,B$ and the characteristic assumption imply $ab=0$ and
$a^2+b^2=1$. Thus $x+J$ is one of $1,-1,B,-B$. Each is represented by a
norm-one element $q$ with $q^*=q^{-1}$. Replacing $x$ by $q^{-1}x$ reduces the
problem to elements congruent to $1$ modulo $J$.

For $m=2$, write $u=1+cN+dBN$. Here $(BN)^*=BN$ and every product of two
terms from $J$ is zero. Hence $u^*u=1+2dBN$, so $d=0$ and $u=E_2(c)$.

For $m=3$, write
$u=1+cN+dBN+eN^2+fBN^2$. The relations give
\[
 u^*u=1+2dBN+(2e-c^2-d^2)N^2+(2f+2cd)BN^2.
\]
The displayed basis is independent. Thus $d=0$, $e=c^2/2$ and $f=0$;
again $u=E_3(c)$. These computations also verify that every element in the
claimed list has norm one. The four possible reductions modulo $J$ distinguish
$\delta$ and $\epsilon$, and independence of $N$ distinguishes $t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:dihedral}
Suppose $K=\F_{11}$, $n$ is odd, $C\subseteq\End_K(K^n)$ satisfies
Theorem~\ref{thm:units}, and $\det B=1$. Then
\[
 \SO(Q)\cap C=\{B^\epsilon E_m(t):\epsilon\in\{0,1\},\ t\in\F_{11}\}.
\]
If $U=E_m(1)$, this group is $\langle B,U\rangle$ and has order $22$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every $E_m(t)$ is unipotent, and therefore has determinant one. Since $n$ is
odd, multiplication by $-1$ changes the determinant. The two negative cosets
in Theorem~\ref{thm:units} are therefore excluded. Truncated multiplication gives
$E_m(s)E_m(t)=E_m(s+t)$ and $BE_m(t)B=E_m(-t)$. Since $\F_{11}$ is prime,
$E_m(t)=U^t$ for the integer representative $0\le t<11$.
The basis assumption implies $N\ne0$ and distinguishes $B$ from these eleven
unipotents. Hence the order is $22$.
\end{proof}

For a non-prime finite field the additive root group would instead have the
order of that field and need not be cyclic. No such cyclic-dihedral assertion
is used by the diagnostic, which is restricted to $\F_{11}$.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:intersection}
In the row-isometry setting above, suppose $n=7$, $B=r_1$, and the old middle
group is the certified group $D=\langle B,U\rangle$ of order $22$.
If the new common-commutant certificate proves Theorem~\ref{thm:units} for
$m=2$ or $3$, and directly checks $U=E_m(1)$, then $H\cap R=D$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The old middle generators belong to both ends, so $D\subseteq H\cap R$.
The reduction to $C$ and Corollary~\ref{cor:dihedral} give the reverse inclusion.
\end{proof}

\section{A certificate with twenty-five unknowns}
Assume that the direct calculation verifies $P_H^2=P_H$ and
$\operatorname{rank}P_H=4$. Choose four independent rows in its image and three
rows in its row kernel; let $S$ have these seven rows. Then
\[
 SP_HS^{-1}=\operatorname{diag}(I_4,0_3).
\]
Every commuting matrix in this frame has two diagonal blocks, of sizes four
and three, and therefore has $16+9=25$ unknown entries. Put
$T=SP_RS^{-1}$. For the allowed block positions $(a,b)$, form a $49\times25$
matrix $M$ whose entry in row $(i,j)$ and column $(a,b)$ is
\[
 \delta_{ia}T_{bj}-T_{ia}\delta_{bj}.
\]
Its kernel is exactly the common commutant in this frame.

For $d=2m\in\{4,6\}$, the following finite data suffice:
\begin{enumerate}
\item complete matrices $Q^{-1},S,S^{-1}$, checked by their full inverse
products, and the full projector and conjugation equations;
\item a nonsingular $(25-d)\times(25-d)$ minor of $M$, with its complete
inverse product checked over $\F_{11}$;
\item the $d$ candidate matrices in $\mathcal B_m$, all commuting with both
projections, and an independent $d\times d$ entry minor with its inverse;
\item all multiplication, adjoint and nilpotence equations in
Theorem~\ref{thm:units}, and $U=E_m(1)$.
\end{enumerate}
The minor in (2) proves that the commutant has dimension at most $d$;
(3) proves that it has dimension at least $d$. Thus the basis assertion is
certified without relying on a guessed normalizer or a classification list.
RREF supplies the minor indices, but the acceptance checks use complete inverse
products. If the dimension or any required relation differs, the output is
inconclusive and retains that diagnostic information.

\section{Attribution and limits}
The row convention, named-group membership, the two old positive tensor
congruence applications, and the complete old dihedral journal are imported
premises. Their successful arithmetic is not reconstructed. The new finite work
checks the candidate form and special-isometry equations, converts the saved
tensors to projections, and certifies their common commutant and its adjoint
relations. Gaussian elimination and the elementary ring identities are standard
algebra. The contribution here is their combination into this small conditional
intersection certificate. Neither a new Original case nor an open conjecture
is claimed until an actual supported certificate and a separate reviewed
application have been issued.
